 ## Overview
-Shadow Volume Rendering is a specialized ray marching routine that computes only the accumulated alpha (transmittance) along a ray to generate shadow maps or occlusion values for volume scenes [S1]. It precomputes a shadow volume to store the amount of light arriving at each sample after attenuation by intervening material, allowing the renderer to multiply interpolated values from this map by colors from the transfer function during the main pass [S2]. Unlike [Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping](1415-ray-marching-with-shadow-mapping.md), which uses an opacity shadow map for occlusion, this method explicitly constructs a volumetric shadow buffer [S2]. It differs from [Volume Shadow Calculation via Light Strength Buffer](1914-volume-shadow-calculation-via-light-strength-buffer.md) and [Light Injection Pass](2759-light-injection-pass.md) by focusing on precomputing the shadow volume itself rather than accumulating light strength or injecting light [S2].
